# DCHECK(1M)

## NAME

dcheck - file system directory consistency check

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/dcheck** [ **-i** *numbers* ] [ *filesystem* ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Dcheck* reads the directories in a file system and compares the link-count in each i-node with
the number of directory entries by which it is referenced. If the file system is not specified,
**/dev/rmd0** is checked.

The **-i** flag is followed by a list of up to ten i-numbers; when one of those i-numbers turns
up in a directory, the number, the i-number of the directory, and the name of the entry are
reported. **Note:** v7's program stopped at ten as well and its page did not say so.

**Note:** v7 said "the program is fastest if the raw version of the special file is used, since
the i-list is read in large chunks". Neither half holds here. The raw device is the only useful
target - it is what **/dev/rmd0** above is - and the i-list is read **one block at a time**
because *physio*() permits nothing else: a raw transfer must be a whole number of 3072-byte
blocks into a buffer whose word address is a multiple of 512, at a block-aligned offset.

**Note:** a name is printed to **eighteen** characters. *DIRSIZ* is 18 on this machine rather
than v7's 14, a directory entry being four 48-bit words - one of i-number and three of name.

**Note:** v7 counted the entries naming each i-number in **one byte,** saturating at 255 and
masking every comparison, because a PDP-11 filesystem could not hold more. A link count is a
whole word here, so a count above 255 is representable on the disk and is reported as it
stands.

## FILES

/dev/rmd0

## SEE ALSO

icheck(1M), ncheck(1M), clri(1M), fsck(1M), filsys(5)

*fsck*(1M) does this program's job in the course of doing five others, and **repairs** a wrong
link count rather than reporting it. *Dcheck* is worth reaching for when only the link counts
are in question.

Nothing here is a block count, so there is no **BLOCKS** section: the two numbers this program
prints are a count of directory entries and a link count. *icheck*(1M) is the one that reports
blocks.

## DIAGNOSTICS

When a file turns up for which the link-count and the number of directory entries disagree, the
relevant facts are reported. Allocated files which have 0 link-count and no entries are also
listed. The only dangerous situation occurs when there are more entries than links; if entries
are removed, so the link-count drops to 0, the remaining entries point to thin air. They should
be removed. When there are more links than entries, or there is an allocated file with neither
links nor entries, some disk space may be lost but the situation will not degenerate.

`n bad` means a directory entry names an i-number outside the i-list; the directory's own
i-number and the entry's name follow it.

`n bad dir blk` means a directory's own block list names a block outside the data area, and the
rest of that directory is not read. **Note:** this diagnostic is not v7's, which reads the
block anyway and charges whatever it finds there to whatever i-numbers the bytes happen to
spell.

`filesystem: not a filesystem` means the super-block carries no *FS_MAGIC* and `filesystem
geometry mismatch` that it describes a block size, an i-nodes-per-block or an
addresses-per-inode this system does not have. **Note:** v7 had neither check, having no such
fields.

## BUGS

Since *dcheck* is inherently two-pass in nature, extraneous diagnostics may be produced if
applied to active file systems.
